1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://amburger.ios-regensburg.de/index.php?id=1118
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Brunnow, Ernst Philipp Bar. v., managing director since 1871. (1797-08-31 Dresden - 1875-04-11 Darmstadt)
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Johann Ernst v. B., from Courland, Electoral Saxon. major
2 CONT Mother: Maria Elisabeth Philippine, born v. Lieven
2 CONT Wife: Charlotte Sabine v. Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Childhood in Dresden, studied in Leipzig. A Russian diplomat since 1818. Service. 1818-21 companion of the Princes Sturdza on d. European congresses, then b. Chr. H. Gf. v. Lieven . 1823 diploma. Official b. Gf. Voroncov in Odessa
2 CONC . 1828 head of the office v. Peter Gf. v. the Pahlen, then senior administrator d. occupied the Danube principalities, even during d. Peace negotiations in Adrianople. Then b. Diebitsch and 1829-30 b. Orlov in Constantinople. Sinc
2 CONC e 1832, the closest colleague of Gf. Nesselrode and editor. most important documents, including representatives since 1833. Authority in d. Main Censorship Administration. 1835-39 older Ministerial Councilor. 1839-40 ambassador in
2 CONC  Stuttgart, also for Darmstadt. 1840 GehRat. 1840-54 ambassador in London, reached e. Understanding in d. Oriental question, but could d. Britain's entry into the Don't prevent war against Russia. 1855-56 Ambassador b. German fede
2 CONC ral government in Frankfurt/M., then the Russian negotiator on the Paris Peace Conference. WGoRat. 1856-58 envoy in Berlin, but remained in Paris until 1857. 1858-60 envoy and 1860-70 ambassador in London. (1862 Andreas-O.) Was ab
2 CONC le to 1863 d. Poland. Counteract propaganda and appease anti-Russian sentiment in England. 1870 Ambassador b. Napoleon III, left France after his abdication. 1870-74 Ambassador to London. Reached 1871 d. British consent to repeal 
2 CONC d. Neutrality d. Black Sea. 1871 Russian Gf. - Since 1875 i. R. in Darmstadt
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From newspaper [http://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/66038970 The Star Ballarat, Vic Wed 16 Jul 1865 p4]
2 CONT ARRIVAL OF BARON BRUNNOW.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baron de Brunnow arrived at Dover, from Calais, on May 2nd, at nine o'clock, and left for town by the twelve o'clock train. The object of this journey is to present Her Majesty the letters of notification of the accession of Emper
2 CONC or Alexander to the throne of Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-10150/1841-england-wales-census?itemId=8402686-&groupId=51ab23dcdf13915c5980fc6230a29e1d&action=showRecord 1841 Census]
2 CONT * Dover Street, Westminster, Middlesex, England
2 CONT * Baron Brunnow	40 - 44
2 CONT * Baroness Brunnow	30 - 34
2 CONT * Olga Brunnow	15 - 19
2 CONT * Feline Brunnow	 5
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philipp_von_Brunnow Ernst Philipp Graf von Brunnow] (August 31, 1797 Dresden – April 12, 1875 Darmstadt) was a Baltic German diplomat who served in the Russian Empire. Brunnow represented Russia in s
2 CONC everal conferences, and held ambassadorial positions in London (1840–1854), Frankfurt (1855), Berlin (1856), and then returned to London (1858–1874).[2] This article incorporates text from a publication now in the public domain: W
2 CONC ood, James, ed. (1907). The Nuttall Encyclopædia. London and New York: Frederick Warne.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Honours'''
2 CONT * Knight Grand Cross of the Legion of Honour. 
2 CONT * Knight Grand Cross of the Order of Saint Stanislaus.
2 CONT * Knight Grand Cross of the Order of the Red Eagle.
2 CONT * Knight Grand Cross of the Order of the Netherlands Lion.
2 CONT * Commander of the Order of St. Stephen of Hungary.
2 CONT * Order of Saint Vladimir
2 CONT * Order of Saint Anna
2 CONT * Order of the White Eagle.
2 CONT * Imperial Order of Saint Alexander Nevsky
2 CONT * Order of St. Andrew the Apostle the First-Called
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding personal names: Until 1919, Graf was a title, translated as Count, not a first or middle name. The female form is Gräfin. In Germany since 1919, it forms part of family names.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Benjamin E. Smith, ed., The Century Cyclopedia of Names: a Pronouncing and Etymological Dictionary of Names in Geography, Biography, Mythology, History, Ethnology, Art, Archæology, Fiction, Etc. (London: The Century Co., 1904), 
2 CONC 189
2 CONT * British and Foreign State Papers, Volume 57 p.34
2 CONT
